{
 "id": "l07",
 "label": "7",
 "title": "Linkers: -ng, -g and na",
 "summary": "The glue that joins describing words, nouns and other words that belong together",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Choose the right linker: -ng, -g or na",
  "Join an adjective and a noun in either order",
  "Recognise the common word pairs that need a linker",
  "Know the main cases where no linker is used",
  "Repeat an adjective for emphasis"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "What a linker does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A **linker** (also called a ligature) is a small piece of glue that joins two neighbouring words when one describes or modifies the other. It has no meaning of its own. It just tells the listener that the two words go together."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog has three linkers. Which one you use depends **only on the last letter of the first word**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "The first word ends in…",
      "Use",
      "How it looks",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a vowel (a, e, i, o, u)",
       "[[-ng|link]]",
       "added to the end of the word",
       "malaki + ng + bahay = [[malaking bahay|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "the consonant **n**",
       "[[-g|link]]",
       "just g added after the n",
       "mayaman + g + lalaki = [[mayamang lalaki|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "any other consonant",
       "[[na|link]]",
       "a separate small word between the two",
       "mabait + na + bata = [[mabait na bata|noun]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Choosing the linker"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Three recipes",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "After a vowel",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "malaki",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "-ng",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "bahay",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "After n",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "mayaman",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "-g",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "lalaki",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "After another consonant",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "mabait",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "na",
         "r": "link"
        },
        {
         "t": "bata",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Whether the first word is the describing word or the noun, the first word always carries the linker."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Look at the **first** word. Its last letter decides the linker.",
      "-ng after a vowel, -g after n, na after any other consonant.",
      "The -g and the -ng are spelled the same way in the finished word: mayamang, malaking."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Either order, same linker rule",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In Tagalog you can put the describing word before or after the noun. The meaning is the same. The rule for the linker stays the same: it goes after the first word."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Describing word first",
      "Noun first",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[magandang bahay|noun]]",
       "[[bahay na maganda|noun]]",
       "a beautiful house"
      ],
      [
       "[[matabang lalaki|noun]]",
       "[[lalaking mataba|noun]]",
       "a fat man"
      ],
      [
       "[[mayamang lalaki|noun]]",
       "[[lalaking mayaman|noun]]",
       "a rich man"
      ],
      [
       "[[batang babae|noun]]",
       "[[babaeng bata|noun]]",
       "a young girl"
      ],
      [
       "[[hanging malamig|noun]]",
       "[[malamig na hangin|noun]]",
       "a cold wind"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Two orders"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Look at the endings",
     "t": "Take **hangin** (wind) and **malamig** (cold). If hangin is first, it ends in n, so use -g: **hanging malamig**. If malamig is first, it ends in g, a consonant other than n, so use na: **malamig na hangin**. The first word always decides."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Mabait na bata si Ana.",
       "en": "Ana is a kind child.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mayamang lalaki si Ben.",
       "en": "Ben is a rich man.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mayaman",
         "rich",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "g",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "lalaki",
         "man",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Masarap na pagkain ito.",
       "en": "This is delicious food.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Masarap",
         "delicious",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "na",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "pagkain",
         "food",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ito",
         "this",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Bagong damit iyon.",
       "en": "That is a new dress.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Bago",
         "new",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ng",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "damit",
         "clothes, dress",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "iyon",
         "that, far away",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Linkers in sentences"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Word pairs that need a linker",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Linkers are not only for adjective and noun. Whenever two words belong together and one modifies the other, you link them. These are the most common pairs."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Pair",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Adjective + noun",
       "[[malaking bahay|noun]]",
       "a big house"
      ],
      [
       "Noun + noun",
       "[[batang babae|noun]]",
       "a young girl (child woman)"
      ],
      [
       "Noun + adjective",
       "[[bahay na maganda|noun]]",
       "a beautiful house"
      ],
      [
       "Adverb + verb",
       "[[mabilis na kumain|pred]]",
       "ate quickly"
      ],
      [
       "Verb + adverb",
       "[[kumaing mabilis|pred]]",
       "ate quickly"
      ],
      [
       "Adjective + verb",
       "[[pagod na dumating|pred]]",
       "arrived tired"
      ],
      [
       "Adverb + adjective",
       "[[totoong mahal|pred]]",
       "truly expensive"
      ],
      [
       "Adjective + adjective (emphasis)",
       "[[malinaw na malinaw|pred]]",
       "very clear"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Common pairs"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "A way to think about it",
     "t": "Listen for the little pause between two linked words. In English the pause is silent (\"big house\"). In Tagalog the linker fills it, like a bridge between the words."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Don't worry about every pair",
     "t": "The verbs in these examples (kumain, dumating) change shape in later lessons. For now, just notice that the linker follows the same rule everywhere."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "When there is no linker",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "There are a few well-known cases where two neighbouring words are **not** linked."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Case",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "The word **kung** (if)",
       "Kung wala pa ako, mauna na kayo.",
       "If I am not here yet, go ahead."
      ],
      [
       "A subject pronoun after a predicate",
       "[[Marunong ka.|pred]], [[Mabait kayo.|pred]]",
       "You are clever. You (plural) are kind."
      ],
      [
       "A possessive word after a noun",
       "[[aklat ko|noun]], [[bahay namin|noun]]",
       "my book, our house"
      ],
      [
       "Most verb + pronoun pairs",
       "[[Liligaya tayo.|pred]]",
       "We shall be happy."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "No linker needed"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Several adjectives in a row"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When two or more adjectives come before a noun, only the **last** adjective takes the linker."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Mabait at matalinong bata si Ana.",
       "en": "Ana is a kind and intelligent child.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "matalino",
         "intelligent",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ng",
         "linker",
         "link"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Kung needs no linker.",
      "A subject pronoun straight after the predicate is not linked.",
      "A possessive word after a noun is not linked.",
      "With several adjectives, only the last one takes the linker."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Repeating for emphasis",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "To say \"very\", Tagalog often repeats the describing word and links the two copies with **na**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Normal",
      "Emphatic",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[mabait|pred]]",
       "[[mabait na mabait|pred]]",
       "very kind"
      ],
      [
       "[[malinaw|pred]]",
       "[[malinaw na malinaw|pred]]",
       "very clear"
      ],
      [
       "[[masarap|pred]]",
